            <ArticleTitle>Pulmonary embolism following coronary artery bypass grafting.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>181-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/j.1540-8191.2010.01195.x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Pulmonary embolism (PE) is a life-threatening complication, with a deceptive clinical presentation following coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We identified 13 studies on PE in post-CABG patients, spanning a period of 34 years.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The overall cumulative incidence of PE following CABG was 1.3% (111 PEs in 8553 CABGs).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">We suggest further prospective randomized studies to examine the effect of saphenous system vein grafting, and choice of low molecular weight heparin prophylaxis on the incidence of post-CABG PE.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2011 Wiley Periodicals, Inc.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
